# What `docker build` must not copy into this Project's image.
#
# `Dockerfile` starts with `COPY . .`, so without this file the build context is whatever
# happens to be in the directory — including a `node_modules` installed on the host. That is
# not merely wasteful: native dependencies are built for the machine that installed them, so
# a macOS `node_modules` copied into a Linux image produces a container that installs
# nothing, builds, and then fails at runtime on a binary for the wrong platform.
**/node_modules
**/dist

# Build state and history, none of which the image needs.
**/.devbox
.git
**/*.tsbuildinfo

# The uploads directory the shipped `MediaStorage` writes to. Runtime data rather than build
# input, and `**/`-first because `.gitignore` names it at every depth (ADR-0068).
**/kobai-media

# Prose. `.env.example` is documentation too, but it is the one a Developer copies, and
# leaving it in costs nothing.
*.md
**/.env
**/.env.*
!**/.env.example

# Credentials, which must never arrive as a file. If kobai comes from a private mirror,
# `.npmrc` holds an auth token — and a token copied into a build context is in an image
# layer forever, readable by anyone who can pull the image, however many `rm`s come after
# it. The install still needs the file, so `Dockerfile` mounts it as a build secret that
# exists only for the length of that one command:
#
#     docker build --secret id=npmrc,src=$HOME/.npmrc .
#
# This line is the half that makes an accident impossible; the mount is the half that keeps
# the build working. Both, because either alone is a trap.
.npmrc
